(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_equilibrium=[{id:"neet-2026-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product and pH",q:`In a qualitative analysis, Bi\xB3\u207A is detected by appearance of precipitate of BiO(OH)(s). Calculate pH when the following equilibrium exists at 298 K.
BiO(OH)(s) \u21CC BiO\u207A(aq) + OH\u207B(aq), K = 4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070
(Given: log2 = 0.3010)`,o:["4.699","8.714","9.301","5.286"],c:2,s:`K = [BiO\u207A][OH\u207B]/[BiO(OH)(s)]. The solid has activity 1, so K = [BiO\u207A][OH\u207B].
Let each ion have concentration s. Then K = s\xB2.
s = \u221AK = \u221A(4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070) = 2\xD710\u207B\u2075 M, so [OH\u207B] = 2\xD710\u207B\u2075 M.
[H\u207A] = Kw/[OH\u207B] = 10\u207B\xB9\u2074/(2\xD710\u207B\u2075) = (1/2)\xD710\u207B\u2079.
pH = \u2013log[H\u207A] = 9 + log2 = 9.301.`},{id:"neet-2026-q060",pn:60,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Salt analysis (acetate test)",q:"In a test tube containing a salt, a few drops of dilute H\u2082SO\u2084 was added, which gave colourless vapours having the smell of vinegar. The vapours turned the blue litmus paper red. Identify the correct anion from the following :",o:["Acetate, CH\u2083COO\u207B","Carbonate, CO\u2083\xB2\u207B","Sulphate, SO\u2084\xB2\u207B","Sulphate, S\xB2\u207B"],c:0,s:`Salt + dil. H\u2082SO\u2084 gives CH\u2083COOH vapour, which smells of vinegar.
The vapour is acidic, so it turns blue litmus paper red.
So the anion is acetate, CH\u2083COO\u207B.`},{id:"neet-2026-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Acid-base indicators",q:"Phenolphthalein is used as an indicator for the titration of sodium hydroxide solution against a standard solution of oxalic acid. The colour change that is observed at an alkaline pH close to the equivalence point during this titration is:",o:["pinkish red to yellow","yellow to pinkish red","colourless to pink","pink to colourless"],c:2,s:`Phenolphthalein is colourless before the end point.
Close to the equivalence point (alkaline pH) it is pink.
So the colour change is colourless to pink.`},{id:"neet-2026-q078",pn:78,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Buffer pH, Henderson equation",q:"At 298 K, a certain buffer solution contains equal concentrations of X\u207B and HX, Kb for X\u207B is 10\u207B\xB9\u2070. What is the pH of this buffer solution?",o:["2","10","4","6"],c:2,s:`HX \u21CC H\u207A + X\u207B
Ka \xD7 Kb = Kw
Ka = Kw/Kb = 10\u207B\xB9\u2074/10\u207B\xB9\u2070 = 10\u207B\u2074
pKa = 4
pH = pKa + log([X\u207B]/[HX])
Given [X\u207B] = [HX], so log(1) = 0.
pH = 4 + 0 = 4`},{id:"neet-2026r-q086",pn:86,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product and solubility",q:`The correct order of solubility of the given salts in water at 298 K is
Salt \u2014 Ksp at 298 K
AgBr \u2014 5.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3
Zn(OH)\u2082 \u2014 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075
Hg\u2082Cl\u2082 \u2014 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078`,o:["AgBr > Zn(OH)\u2082 > Hg\u2082Cl\u2082","Hg\u2082Cl\u2082 > AgBr > Zn(OH)\u2082","Zn(OH)\u2082 > AgBr > Hg\u2082Cl\u2082","Hg\u2082Cl\u2082 > Zn(OH)\u2082 > AgBr"],c:2,s:`AgBr(s) \u21CC Ag\u207A(aq) + Br\u207B(aq).
Ksp = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 = S\xB2, so S = \u221A(5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3) = 7.07 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 mol/L.
Zn(OH)\u2082(s) \u21CC Zn\xB2\u207A(aq) + 2OH\u207B(aq).
Ksp = 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 = (S)(2S)\xB2 = 4S\xB3, so S = 0.63 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 = 6.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 mol/L.
Hg\u2082Cl\u2082(s) \u21CC Hg\u2082\xB2\u207A(aq) + 2Cl\u207B(aq).
Ksp = 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 = 4S\xB3, so S = 0.69 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 6.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 mol/L.
Correct order of solubility: Zn(OH)\u2082 > AgBr > Hg\u2082Cl\u2082.`},{id:"neet-2026-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Kp and Kc relation",q:"Given below are certain reactions. Identify the reaction for which KP \u2260 KC.",o:["H\u2082(g) + I\u2082(g) \u21CC 2HI(g)","N\u2082(g) + O\u2082(g) \u21CC 2NO(g)","N\u2082(g) + 3H\u2082(g) \u21CC 2NH\u2083(g)","H\u2082O(g) + CO(g) \u21CC H\u2082(g) + CO\u2082(g)"],c:2,s:`KP = KC(RT)^\u0394ng.
KP = KC when \u0394ng = 0, and KP \u2260 KC when \u0394ng \u2260 0.
(1) H\u2082 + I\u2082 \u21CC 2HI: \u0394ng = 0.
(2) N\u2082 + O\u2082 \u21CC 2NO: \u0394ng = 0.
(3) N\u2082 + 3H\u2082 \u21CC 2NH\u2083: \u0394ng = \u20132, so KP \u2260 KC.
(4) H\u2082O + CO \u21CC H\u2082 + CO\u2082: \u0394ng = 0.`},{id:"neet-2025-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Cation analysis groups",q:`Match List I with List II
List-I (Ion) \u2014 List-II (Group Number in Cation Analysis)
A. Co\xB2\u207A \u2014 I. Group-I
B. Mg\xB2\u207A \u2014 II. Group-III
C. Pb\xB2\u207A \u2014 III. Group-IV
D. Al\xB3\u207A \u2014 IV. Group-VI
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],c:1,s:`Co\xB2\u207A is in Group-IV.
Mg\xB2\u207A is in Group-VI.
Pb\xB2\u207A is in Group-I.
Al\xB3\u207A is in Group-III.
So A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2025-q059",pn:59,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Ionisation of polyprotic acids",q:`Phosphoric acid ionizes in three steps with their ionization constant values Ka\u2081, Ka\u2082 and Ka\u2083, respectively, while K is the overall ionization constant. Which of the following statements are true?
A. log K = log Ka\u2081 + log Ka\u2082 + log Ka\u2083
B. H\u2083PO\u2084 is a stronger acid than H\u2082PO\u2084\u207B and HPO\u2084\xB2\u207B
C. Ka\u2081 > Ka\u2082 > Ka\u2083
D. Ka\u2081 = (Ka\u2083 + Ka\u2082)/2
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A and B only","A and C only","B, C and D only","A, B and C only"],c:3,s:`H\u2083PO\u2084 is a stronger acid than H\u2082PO\u2084\u207B and HPO\u2084\xB2\u207B.
H\u2083PO\u2084(aq) \u21CC H\u207A(aq) + H\u2082PO\u2084\u207B(aq), Ka\u2081 = 7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3.
H\u2082PO\u2084\u207B(aq) \u21CC H\u207A(aq) + HPO\u2084\xB2\u207B(aq), Ka\u2082 = 6.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078.
HPO\u2084\xB2\u207B(aq) \u21CC H\u207A(aq) + PO\u2084\xB3\u207B(aq), Ka\u2083 = 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2.
So Ka\u2081 > Ka\u2082 > Ka\u2083.
K = Ka\u2081 \xD7 Ka\u2082 \xD7 Ka\u2083, so log K = log Ka\u2081 + log Ka\u2082 + log Ka\u2083.
Answer: A, B and C only.`},{id:"neet-2025-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Kp and Kc relation",q:`For the reaction A(g) \u21CC 2B(g), the backward reaction rate constant is higher than the forward reaction rate constant by a factor of 2500, at 1000 K.
[Given : R = 0.0831 L atm mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9]
KP for the reaction at 1000 K is`,o:["83.1","2.077 \xD7 10\u2075","0.033","0.021"],c:2,s:`Kc = k_f/k_b = 1/2500
Kp = Kc(RT)^\u0394n_g, where \u0394n_g = 2 \u2013 1 = 1
= (1/2500) \xD7 0.0831 \xD7 1000
= 0.033`},{id:"neet-2025-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Le Chatelier's principle",q:`Higher yield of NO in N\u2082(g) + O\u2082(g) \u21CC 2NO(g) can be obtained at
[\u0394H of the reaction = +180.7 kJ mol\u207B\xB9]
A. Higher temperature
B. Lower temperature
C. Higher concentration of N\u2082
D. Higher concentration of O\u2082
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A, D only","B, C only","B, C, D only","A, C, D only"],c:3,s:`Yield of the product generally depends on temperature, concentration of reactant(s) and product(s), and pressure.
This is an endothermic reaction (\u0394H = +180.7 kJ mol\u207B\xB9), so an increase in temperature shifts the equilibrium in the forward direction and increases the yield of NO.
Increase in concentration of reactants (N\u2082 and O\u2082) also shifts the equilibrium in the forward direction and increases the yield of NO.
Hence, (A), (C) and (D) only will increase the yield of NO.`},{id:"neet-2024-q072",pn:72,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Reaction quotient",q:`For the reaction 2A \u21CC B + C, K_C = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3. At a given time, the composition of reaction mixture is : [A] = [B] = [C] = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 M.
Then which of the following is correct?`,o:["Reaction has a tendency to go in backward direction","Reaction has gone to completion in forward direction","Reaction is at equilibrium.","Reaction has a tendency to go in forward direction"],c:0,s:`Find the reaction quotient Q_C and compare it with K_C.
Q_C = [B][C]/[A]\xB2 = (2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)(2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 1.
Q_C = 1 is greater than K_C = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3.
So the reaction tends to move in the backward direction.`},{id:"neet-2024-q074",pn:74,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Relation between Kp and Kc",q:"In which of the following equilibria, Kp and Kc are NOT equal ?",o:["CO(g) + H\u2082O(g) \u21CC CO\u2082(g) + H\u2082(g)","2BrCl(g) \u21CC Br\u2082(g) + Cl\u2082(g)","PCl\u2085(g) \u21CC PCl\u2083(g) + Cl\u2082(g)","H\u2082(g) + I\u2082(g) \u21CC 2HI(g)"],c:2,s:`K_p = K_c (RT)^(\u0394n_g).
K_p differs from K_c only when \u0394n_g \u2260 0, where \u0394n_g = moles of gaseous products \u2013 moles of gaseous reactants.
(1) \u0394n_g = 2 \u2013 2 = 0.
(2) \u0394n_g = 2 \u2013 2 = 0.
(3) \u0394n_g = 2 \u2013 1 = 1.
(4) \u0394n_g = 2 \u2013 2 = 0.
So only option (3) has K_p \u2260 K_c.`},{id:"neet-2024-q093",pn:93,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Qualitative analysis cation groups",q:`Given below are certain cations. Using inorganic qualitative analysis, arrange them in increasing group number from 0 to VI.
A. Al\xB3\u207A
B. Cu\xB2\u207A
C. Ba\xB2\u207A
D. Co\xB2\u207A
E. Mg\xB2\u207A
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["E, C, D, B, A","E, A, B, C, D","B, A, D, C, E","B, C, A, D, E"],c:2,s:`Groups of the cations: Cu\xB2\u207A is Group II, Al\xB3\u207A is Group III, Co\xB2\u207A is Group IV, Ba\xB2\u207A is Group V, Mg\xB2\u207A is Group VI.
Increasing group number: Cu\xB2\u207A (B) < Al\xB3\u207A (A) < Co\xB2\u207A (D) < Ba\xB2\u207A (C) < Mg\xB2\u207A (E).
The order is B, A, D, C, E.`,sfig:{w:200,h:152,alt:"Table of groups and cations: Group II Cu2+, Group III Al3+, Group IV Co2+, Group V Ba2+, Group VI Mg2+.",d:[["r",10,10,180,134],["l",100,10,100,144],["l",10,34,190,34],["l",10,56,190,56],["l",10,78,190,78],["l",10,100,190,100],["l",10,122,190,122],["t",55,26,"Group","m"],["t",145,26,"Cations","m"],["t",55,49,"Group-II","m"],["t",145,49,"Cu\xB2\u207A","m"],["t",55,71,"Group-III","m"],["t",145,71,"Al\xB3\u207A","m"],["t",55,93,"Group-IV","m"],["t",145,93,"Co\xB2\u207A","m"],["t",55,115,"Group-V","m"],["t",145,115,"Ba\xB2\u207A","m"],["t",55,137,"Group-VI","m"],["t",145,137,"Mg\xB2\u207A","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q096",pn:96,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Degree of dissociation from Kc",q:`Consider the following reaction in a sealed vessel at equilibrium with concentrations of N\u2082 = 3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 M, O\u2082 = 4.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 M and NO = 2.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 M.
2NO(g) \u21CC N\u2082(g) + O\u2082(g)
If 0.1 mol L\u207B\xB9 of NO(g) is taken in closed vessel, what will be degree of dissociation (\u03B1) of NO(g) at equilibrium ?`,o:["0.8889","0.717","0.00889","0.0889"],c:1,s:`Kc = [N\u2082][O\u2082]/[NO]\xB2.
Kc = (3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 4.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(2.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 2.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 1.607.
Start: NO = 0.1, N\u2082 = 0, O\u2082 = 0.
At equilibrium: NO = 0.1 \u2013 0.1\u03B1, N\u2082 = 0.05\u03B1, O\u2082 = 0.05\u03B1.
Kc = (0.05\u03B1 \xD7 0.05\u03B1)/(0.1 \u2013 0.1\u03B1)\xB2 = (0.05)\xB2\u03B1\xB2/(0.01(1 \u2013 \u03B1)\xB2).
So \u03B1\xB2/(1 \u2013 \u03B1)\xB2 = 1.607 \xD7 (0.1)\xB2/(0.05)\xB2.
Taking the square root: \u03B1/(1 \u2013 \u03B1) = 1.27 \xD7 0.1/0.05 = 2.54.
\u03B1 = 2.54 \u2013 2.54\u03B1.
3.54\u03B1 = 2.54.
\u03B1 = 2.54/3.54 = 0.717.`},{id:"neet-2023-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Lewis acids and bases",q:"Amongst the given options which of the following molecules/ion acts as a Lewis acid ?",o:["OH\u207B","NH\u2083","H\u2082O","BF\u2083"],c:3,s:`A Lewis acid accepts a lone pair of electrons.
It needs a vacant orbital in its outer shell to do so.
BF\u2083 has a vacant orbital on boron, so it is a Lewis acid.
H\u2082O, OH\u207B and NH\u2083 have lone pairs to donate, so they are Lewis bases.`},{id:"neet-2023-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Gibbs energy and equilibrium constant",q:"The equilibrium concentrations of the species in the reaction A + B \u21CC C + D are 2, 3, 10 and 6 mol L\u207B\xB9, respectively at 300 K. \u0394G\xB0 for the reaction is (R = 2 cal/mol K)",o:["\u201313.73 cal","\u20131372.60 cal","\u2013137.26 cal","\u20131381.80 cal"],c:3,s:`K = [C][D]/([A][B]) = (10 \xD7 6)/(2 \xD7 3) = 10.
\u0394G\xB0 = \u2013RT ln K = \u20132.303 RT log K.
= \u20132.303 \xD7 2 \xD7 300 \xD7 log 10.
= \u20131381.8 cal.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q067",pn:67,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"pH of weak acid",q:"0.01 M acetic acid solution is 1% ionised, then pH of this acetic acid solution is :",o:["1","3","2","4"],c:3,s:`CH\u2083COOH \u21CC CH\u2083COO\u207B + H\u207A.
At equilibrium [H\u207A] = C\u03B1.
[H\u207A] = 0.01 \xD7 (1/100) = 10\u207B\u2074 M.
pH = \u2013log[H\u207A] = 4.`},{id:"neet-2022-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Buffer solutions",q:"The pH of the solution containing 50 mL each of 0.10 M sodium acetate and 0.01 M acetic acid is [Given pKa of CH\u2083COOH = 4.57]",o:["3.57","4.57","2.57","5.57"],c:3,s:`The mixture is a weak acid with the salt of its conjugate base, so it is an acidic buffer.
pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]).
Equal volumes are mixed, so the ratio stays 0.10/0.01 = 10.
pH = 4.57 + log 10 = 4.57 + 1 = 5.57.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q090",pn:90,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Relation between Kp and Kc",q:`Kp for the following reaction is 3.0 at 1000 K.
CO\u2082(g) + C(s) \u21CC 2CO(g)
What will be the value of Kc for the reaction at the same temperature ?
(Given \u2013 R = 0.083 L bar K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9)`,o:["3.6","0.36","3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2","3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3"],c:2,s:`Kp = Kc(RT)^\u0394ng.
\u0394ng = 2 - 1 = 1 (only gases count).
3 = Kc \xD7 (0.083 \xD7 1000).
Kc = 3/(0.083 \xD7 1000) = 3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2022-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Equilibrium constant Kc",q:`3O\u2082(g) \u21CC 2O\u2083(g)
for the above reaction at 298 K, Kc is found to be 3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075\u2079. If the concentration of O\u2082 at equilibrium is 0.040 M then concentration of O\u2083 in M is`,o:["1.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076\xB3","2.4 \xD7 10\xB3\xB9","1.2 \xD7 10\xB2\xB9","4.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB2"],c:3,s:`Kc = [O\u2083]\xB2/[O\u2082]\xB3.
[O\u2083]\xB2 = Kc \xD7 [O\u2082]\xB3 = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075\u2079 \xD7 (0.04)\xB3 = 1.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076\xB3 = 19 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076\u2074.
[O\u2083] = \u221A(19 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076\u2074) = 4.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB2 M.`},{id:"neet-2021-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"pH of salt of weak acid and weak base",q:"The pKb of dimethylamine and pKa of acetic acid are 3.27 and 4.77 respectively at T (K). The correct option for the pH of dimethylammonium acetate solution is :",o:["6.25","8.50","5.50","7.75"],c:3,s:`The salt comes from a weak acid and a weak base, so pH = 7 + (1/2)(pKa \u2013 pKb).
= 7 + (1/2)(4.77 \u2013 3.27) = 7 + 0.75.
pH = 7.75.`},{id:"neet-2020-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product",q:"Find out the solubility of Ni(OH)\u2082 in 0.1 M NaOH. Given that the ionic product of Ni(OH)\u2082 is 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075",o:["2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 M","1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 M","1 \xD7 10\u2078 M","2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 M"],c:3,s:`NaOH gives 0.1 M OH\u207B.
Let the solubility of Ni(OH)\u2082 be S: [Ni\xB2\u207A] = S, [OH\u207B] = 0.1 + 2S \u2248 0.1.
K = S(0.1)\xB2 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075.
S = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075/10\u207B\xB2 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 M.`},{id:"neet-2020-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Common ion effect",q:"HCl was passed through solution of CaCl\u2082, MgCl\u2082 and NaCl. Which of the following compound(s) crystallise(s) ?",o:["Only NaCl","Only MgCl\u2082","NaCl, MgCl\u2082 and CaCl\u2082","Both MgCl\u2082 and CaCl\u2082"],c:0,s:`HCl adds Cl\u207B ions, raising [Cl\u207B].
The ionic product [Na\u207A][Cl\u207B] first exceeds the solubility product of NaCl, so NaCl separates (common ion effect).
MgCl\u2082 and CaCl\u2082 are much more soluble and stay in solution.`},{id:"neet-2019-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product",q:"pH of a saturated solution of Ca(OH)\u2082 is 9. The solubility product (Ksp) of Ca(OH)\u2082 is -",o:["0.125 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075","0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070","0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075","0.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070"],c:2,s:`pH = 9, so pOH = 5 and [OH\u207B] = 10\u207B\u2075 M.
Ca(OH)\u2082 \u21CC Ca\xB2\u207A + 2OH\u207B; if s is the solubility, [OH\u207B] = 2s, so s = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 M.
Ksp = s \xD7 (2s)\xB2 = (0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)(10\u207B\u2075)\xB2 = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075.`},{id:"neet-2019-q174",pn:174,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Br\xF8nsted acids and bases",q:"Conjugate base for Bronsted acids H\u2082O and HF are :",o:["OH\u207B and F\u207B, respectively","H\u2083O\u207A and H\u2082F\u207A, respectively","OH\u207B and H\u2082F\u207A, respectively","H\u2083O\u207A and F\u207B, respectively"],c:0,s:`A conjugate base is formed when the acid loses H\u207A.
H\u2082O \u2212 H\u207A = OH\u207B.
HF \u2212 H\u207A = F\u207B.`},{id:"neet-2019-q175",pn:175,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Buffer solutions",q:"Which will make basic buffer?",o:["100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 200 mL of 0.1 M NH\u2084OH","100 mL of 0.1 M HCl + 100 mL of 0.1 M NH\u2084OH","50 mL of 0.1 M NaOH + 25 mL of 0.1 M CH\u2083COOH","100 mL of 0.1 M CH\u2083COOH + 100 mL of 0.1 M NaOH"],c:0,s:`A basic buffer is a weak base plus its salt, so the weak base must be left over after the strong acid is added.
Option 1: NH\u2084OH = 200 \xD7 0.1 = 20 mmol, HCl = 100 \xD7 0.1 = 10 mmol. After reaction 10 mmol NH\u2084OH is left with 10 mmol NH\u2084Cl: a basic buffer.
Option 2: 10 mmol each, so only salt (NH\u2084Cl) is left: no buffer.
Option 3: NaOH = 5 mmol, CH\u2083COOH = 2.5 mmol; the acid is used up and 2.5 mmol strong base is left over: not a buffer.
Option 4: 10 mmol each, only CH\u2083COONa is left: not a buffer.
Only option 1 is a basic buffer.
Note: Option 2 is printed 'NHOH' in the source; read as NH\u2084OH.`},{id:"neet-2018-q069",pn:69,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"pH of mixed acid and base",q:"Following solutions were prepared by mixing different volumes of NaOH and HCl of different concentrations : (a) 60 mL (M)/(10) HCl + 40 mL (M)/(10) NaOH (b) 55 mL (M)/(10) HCl + 45 mL (M)/(10) NaOH (c) 75 mL (M)/(5) HCl + 25 mL (M)/(5) NaOH (d) 100 mL (M)/(10) HCl + 100 mL (M)/(10) NaOH. pH of which one of them will be equal to 1 ?",o:["b","a","d","c"],c:3,s:`Find the excess H\u207A after mixing and divide by total volume.
(a) 6 \u2013 4 = 2 meq in 100 mL: 0.02 M, pH = 1.7.
(b) 5.5 \u2013 4.5 = 1 meq in 100 mL: 0.01 M, pH = 2.
(c) 15 \u2013 5 = 10 meq in 100 mL: 0.1 M, pH = 1.
(d) 10 \u2013 10 = 0: neutral, pH = 7.
So (c).`},{id:"neet-2018-q070",pn:70,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product",q:"The solubility of BaSO\u2084 in water is 2.42 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 gL\u207B\xB9 at 298 K. The value of its solubility product (Ksp) will be (Given molar mass of BaSO\u2084 = 233 g mol\u207B\xB9)",o:["1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2","1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2","1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2","1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2"],c:0,s:`Convert to mol/L: S = (2.42 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(233) = 1.04 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 mol/L.
BaSO\u2084 \u21CC Ba\xB2\u207A + SO\u2084\xB2\u207B, so Ksp = S \xD7 S = S\xB2.
Ksp = (1.04 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)\xB2 = 1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 mol\xB2 L\u207B\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2018-q077",pn:77,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Le Chatelier's principle",q:"Which one of the following conditions will favour maximum formation of the product in the reaction A\u2082(g) + B\u2082(g) \u21CC X\u2082(g) \u0394rH = \u2013 X kJ ?",o:["Low temperature and high pressure","Low temperature and low pressure","High temperature and high pressure","High temperature and low pressure"],c:0,s:`The reaction is exothermic (\u0394H negative), so low temperature favours the forward reaction.
Moles of gas fall from 2 to 1 (\u0394ng < 0), so high pressure favours the forward reaction.
Best condition: low temperature and high pressure.`},{id:"neet-2017-q009",pn:9,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product",q:"Concentration of the Ag\u207A ions in a saturated solution of Ag2C2O4 is 2.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 mol L\u207B\xB9. Solubility product of Ag2C2O4 is :",o:["5.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2","2.42 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078","2.66 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2","4.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9"],c:0,s:`Ag2C2O4 \u21CC 2Ag\u207A + C2O4\xB2\u207B. If solubility is s, [Ag\u207A] = 2s and [C2O4\xB2\u207B] = s.
2s = 2.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074, so s = 1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 mol/L.
Ksp = (2s)\xB2 \xD7 s = 4s\xB3.
Ksp = 4 \xD7 (1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)\xB3 = 4 \xD7 1.331 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 = 5.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2017-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Combining equilibrium constants",q:`The equilibrium constants of the following are :
N2 + 3H2 \u21CC 2NH3 (K1)
N2 + O2 \u21CC 2NO (K2)
H2 + \xBDO2 \u2192 H2O (K3)
The equilibrium constant (K) of the reaction : 2NH3 + (5)/(2) O2 \u21CC 2NO + 3H2O, will be :`,o:["K2\xB2 K3/K1","K1 K3\xB3/K2","K2 K3\xB3/K1","K2 K3/K1"],c:2,s:`Target: 2NH3 + 5/2 O2 gives 2NO + 3H2O.
Reverse equation 1 (K becomes 1/K1): 2NH3 gives N2 + 3H2.
Add equation 2 (K2): N2 + O2 gives 2NO.
Add 3 \xD7 equation 3 (K becomes K3\xB3): 3H2 + 3/2 O2 gives 3H2O.
Adding: 2NH3 + 5/2 O2 gives 2NO + 3H2O.
When reactions are added, K values multiply: K = K2 \xD7 K3\xB3 / K1.`},{id:"neet-2017-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Kp and Boyle's law",q:`A 20 litre container at 400 K contains CO2(g) at pressure 0.4 atm and an excess of SrO (neglect the volume of solid SrO). The volume of the container is now decreased by moving the movable piston fitted in the container. The maximum volume of the container, when pressure of CO2 attains its maximum value, will be :
(Given that : SrCO3(s) \u21CC SrO(s) + CO2(g), Kp = 1.6 atm)`,o:["2 litre","5 litre","10 litre","4 litre"],c:1,s:`For SrCO3(s) \u21CC SrO(s) + CO2(g), Kp = P(CO2).
So the highest pressure CO2 can have is 1.6 atm; beyond that SrCO3 forms.
Up to this point only the gas is compressed at constant T, so P1V1 = P2V2.
0.4 \xD7 20 = 1.6 \xD7 V2, so V2 = 5 L.`},{id:"neet-2017-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Catalyst and equilibrium",q:"Which one of the following statements is not correct ?",o:["Coenzymes increase the catalytic activity of enzyme","Catalyst does not initiate and reaction","The value of equilibrium constant is changed in the presence of a catalyst in the reaction at equilibrium .","Enzymes catalyse mainly bio-chemical reaction."],c:2,s:`A catalyst speeds up the forward and backward reactions equally.
So it only helps the system reach equilibrium sooner; it does not change K.
The statement that K changes with a catalyst is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2016-q034",pn:34,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product",q:"MY and NY\u2083, two nearly insoluble salts, have the same K_SP values of 6.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 at room temperature. Which statement would be true in regard to MY and NY\u2083 ?",o:["The molar solubility of MY in water is less than that of NY\u2083","The salts MY and NY\u2083 are more soluble in 0.5 M KY than in pure water","The addition of the salt of KY to solution of MY and NY\u2083 will have no effect on their solubilities","The molar solubilities of MY and NY\u2083 in water are identical"],c:0,s:`For MY: K_sp = S\xB2, so S = \u221A(6.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3) = \u221A(62 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074) \u2248 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 M.
For NY\u2083 \u21CC N\xB3\u207A + 3Y\u207B: K_sp = S \xD7 (3S)\xB3 = 27S\u2074.
S = (6.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3/27)^(1/4) = (0.2296 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3)^(1/4) \u2248 3.89 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 M.
So MY is less soluble than NY\u2083, option (1).
KY adds common ion Y\u207B, which lowers solubility, so (2) and (3) are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2016-q041",pn:41,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 1 May",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Clausius-Clapeyron equation",q:"Consider the following liquid-vapour equilibrium: Liquid \u21CC Vapour. Which of the following relations is correct ?",o:["d ln P/dT = -\u0394H_v/RT","d ln P/dT\xB2 = -\u0394H_v/T\xB2","d ln P/dT = \u0394H_v/RT\xB2","d ln G/dT\xB2 = \u0394H_v/RT\xB2"],c:2,s:`Clausius-Clapeyron: P = A\xB7e^(-\u0394H_v/RT).
Take ln: ln P = ln A - \u0394H_v/(RT).
Differentiate with respect to T: d ln P/dT = 0 + (\u0394H_v/R)\xB7d(-T\u207B\xB9)... that is, d/dT(-1/T) = +1/T\xB2.
So d ln P/dT = \u0394H_v/(RT\xB2), option (3).`},{id:"neet-2016b-q054",pn:54,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Degree of ionisation of a weak base",q:"The percentage of pyridine (C\u2085H\u2085N) that forms pyridinium ion (C\u2085H\u2085N\u207AH) in a 0.10 M aqueous pyridine solution (Kb for C\u2085H\u2085N = 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079) is",o:["0.0060%","0.013%","0.77%","1.6%"],c:1,s:`For a weak base, degree of ionisation \u03B1 = \u221A(Kb/C).
\u03B1 = \u221A(1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079/0.10) = \u221A(1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078).
\u03B1 = 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074.
Percentage = 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 100 = 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 % = 0.013%.`},{id:"neet-2016b-q058",pn:58,y:"AIPMT 2016 \xB7 24 Jul",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Common ion effect on solubility",q:"The solubility of AgCl (s) with solubility product 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 in 0.1 M NaCl solution would be :",o:["1.26 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 M","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 M","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 M","zero"],c:1,s:`NaCl is fully ionised, so [Cl\u207B] from NaCl = 0.1 M.
Let the solubility of AgCl be S, so [Ag\u207A] = S.
The Cl\u207B from AgCl is tiny compared with 0.1 M, so [Cl\u207B] \u2248 0.1.
Ksp = [Ag\u207A][Cl\u207B] = S \xD7 0.1 = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070.
S = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 M.`}]})();
